}}{{Politics of Ecuador}}

General elections were held in [[Ecuador]] on 17 February 2013 to elect the [[President of Ecuador|President]], the [[National Assembly of Ecuador|National Assembly]], Provincial Assemblies and members of the [[Andean Parliament]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://andes.info.ec/english/8486.html |title=To present, 85 institutions are authorized by the National Electoral Council to issue publications {{!}} ANDES<!-- Bot generated title --> |access-date=2012-11-05 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20130212195638/http://andes.info.ec/english/8486.html |archive-date=2013-02-12 |url-status=dead }}</ref> The incumbent President [[Rafael Correa]] was re-elected by a wide margin.<ref>{{cite news|last=Neuman|first=William|title=President Correa Handily Wins Re-election in Ecuador|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2013/02/18/world/americas/rafael-correa-wins-re-election-in-ecuador.html?_r=0|newspaper=The New York Times|date=17 February 2013}}</ref> Correa's closest electoral rival, [[Guillermo Lasso]], conceded the election shortly after it concluded.

The vote had been set for January 2013, but was put back a month to allow a full year to elapse after the reform of election rules.<ref>{{cite news| url=http://uk.reuters.com/article/ecuador-election-idUKL2E8DOELU20120224 | work=Reuters | title=Ecuador's presidential election set for February 2013 | date=24 February 2012}}{{dead link|date=September 2024|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref>

This was the first election since [[1996 Ecuadorian general election|1996]] held after the natural expiration of a four-year presidential term. This was due to a decade of political and economical instability that Ecuador experienced after [[Abdalá Bucaram]] was impeached by the former [[Congress of Ecuador|Congress]], in late 1997, and that lasted until Correa's inauguration in early 2007.<ref name="LaRepública">[http://www.larepublica.ec/blog/politica/2014/01/17/los-7-anos-de-correa-en-ecuador-estabilidad-en-medio-de-exitos-y-criticas/ "Los 7 años de Correa en Ecuador: estabilidad en medio de éxitos y críticas"] 17 January 2014. ''La República''</ref>

==Background==
The call for general elections occurred on 18 October 2012. The election campaigns were officially launched on 4 January and ended on 14 February, as announced by CNE member Juan Pablo Pozo.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://andes.info.ec/english/6231.html |title=Ecuador will convene for elections on October 18 {{!}} ANDES<!-- Bot generated title --> |access-date=2012-10-01 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20130212200212/http://andes.info.ec/english/6231.html |archive-date=2013-02-12 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

Voters chose the president and vice president of the Republic, as well as national and provincial assembly members, members of the Andean Parliament, mayors, prefects, and other sectional officials.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.ecuadortimes.net/2011/09/02/cne-introduced-2013-election-calendar/ |title=Ecuador Times - CNE Introduced 2013 Election Calendar |access-date=2012-06-02 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304193354/http://www.ecuadortimes.net/2011/09/02/cne-introduced-2013-election-calendar/ |archive-date=2016-03-04 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

The seated President Correa, a two-term incumbent, is credited with bringing stability and prosperity to Ecuador's nearly 15 million people. A staunch friend of [[Venezuela]]'s [[Hugo Chavez]], he is usually described as "leftist" and "socialist." He is committed to poverty reduction through not just economic growth, but [[redistribution of wealth]] as well. As he told the CELAC Summit in 2015: 
"For the first time in the history of humanity this poverty is no longer due to a lack of resources, but rather is the fruit of inequality, and this, in turn, the product of perverse relations of power, where few have it all and many have nothing at all.”<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.importantcool.com/sydney-to-celac-the-poverty-of-western-politics-and-the-rise-of-the-third-world/|title = Nothing2Hide – Just another WordPress site}}</ref>
This approach has generated fervent support as well as hostility from wealthy sectors in Ecuador; for example, his [[land reform]] policies for giving unproductive land to poor peasants has worried agribusiness, particularly the large flower and banana producers. He has vastly expanded the number of workers on the [[Civil service|public sector]] and made health and education free for all Ecuadorians. Some have argued that Correa has clamped down on dissent in the country's [[news media]], although journalists in Ecuador are not risking their lives as they are in other countries of the region.<ref>{{cite news|last=Garcia |first=Eduardo |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-ecuador-election-idUSBRE91H0NL20130218 |title=Ecuador's re-elected Correa vows media and land reforms |publisher=Reuters |date=2013-02-18 |access-date=2013-02-22}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|author=Associated Press in Quito |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2013/feb/18/ecuador-president-heralds-citizen-revolution |title=Ecuador's President Rafael Correa says citizens will be in charge, not money &#124; World news &#124; guardian.co.uk |publisher=Guardian |date=2013-02-18 |access-date=2013-02-22 |location=London}}</ref> His government has run record-high deficits. In 2009, Correa became the first president of Ecuador to win re-election in nearly a half-century.<ref name="Gill">{{cite news|last=Gill |first=Nathan |url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/2013-02-18/correa-re-elected-in-ecuador-on-vow-to-spend-amid-record-deficit.html |title=Ecuador Re-Elects Correa on Vow to Spend Amid Record Deficit |publisher=Bloomberg |access-date=2013-02-22}}</ref>

===Democracy Code===
In late 2012, Ecuador's Constitutional Court (CC) ruled on the adoption of the Democracy Code by a 6 to 3 vote in favor. The new resolution made the [[D'Hondt method]] of voting constitutional; therefore it was applied in the 2013 elections. The resolution also states that legal restrictions on the work of journalists during elections are unconstitutional.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.ecuadortimes.net/2012/10/18/constitutional-court-resolves-democracy-code/ |title=Constitutional Court resolves Democracy Code {{!}} EcuadorTimes.net {{!}} Breaking News, Ecuador News, World, Sports, Entertainment<!-- Bot generated title --> |access-date=2012-10-19 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121019153726/http://www.ecuadortimes.net/2012/10/18/constitutional-court-resolves-democracy-code/ |archive-date=2012-10-19 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

==Presidential elections==

===Candidates===
Eight presidential candidates were registered in the National Electoral Council.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.ecuadortimes.net/2013/01/04/the-electoral-race-for-votes-starts-today/ |title=The electoral race for votes starts today {{!}} EcuadorTimes.net {{!}} Breaking News, Ecuador News, World, Sports, Entertainment<!-- Bot generated title --> |access-date=2013-01-07 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130107005942/http://www.ecuadortimes.net/2013/01/04/the-electoral-race-for-votes-starts-today/ |archive-date=2013-01-07 |url-status=dead }}</ref>  The candidates were: [[Rafael Correa]] of the [[PAIS Alliance]] movement, Guillermo Lasso of the [[Creating Opportunities]] movement, [[Lucio Gutiérrez]] of the [[January 21 Patriotic Society Party|PSP]] party, Alberto Acosta Espinoza, candidate of the [[Pachakutik Plurinational Unity Movement – New Country|Coordinating Committee of the Left]], {{ill|Norman Wray (politician)|lt=Norman Wray|es|Norman Wray}}, of the [[Movimiento Construye|Ruptura 25]] movement, Nelson Zavala, of the [[Ecuadorian Roldosist Party|Roldosist Party]], Mauricio Rodas, of the SUMA movement, and [[Álvaro Noboa]], of [[Institutional Renewal Party of National Action|PRIAN]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://andes.info.ec/english/road-and-meetings-eight-candidates-begin-their-race-ecuadorian-presidency.html |title=On the road and in meetings, eight candidates begin their race for the Ecuadorian presidency {{!}} ANDES<!-- Bot generated title --> |access-date=2013-01-07 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130323112023/http://www.andes.info.ec/english/road-and-meetings-eight-candidates-begin-their-race-ecuadorian-presidency.html |archive-date=2013-03-23 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

===Restrictions of the candidate's qualifications===
The National Electoral Council (Spanish-language initials, CNE) approved the regulations for the registration and qualification of candidates running for President, Vice President, Assembly and the Andean Parliament for the election. The regulations established that candidates who have contracts with the State or have received final judgments for offenses penalized with imprisonment such as  bribery, illicit enrichment and embezzlement, would not be allowed to participate.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.ecuadortimes.net/2012/10/03/cne-issues-new-restrictions-for-candidates-qualification/ |title=CNE issues new restrictions for candidates qualification {{!}} EcuadorTimes.net {{!}} Breaking News, Ecuador News, World, Sports, Entertainment<!-- Bot generated title --> |access-date=2012-10-06 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304142644/http://www.ecuadortimes.net/2012/10/03/cne-issues-new-restrictions-for-candidates-qualification/ |archive-date=2016-03-04 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

==Parliamentary elections==
Ecuadorians not only voted on 17 February for their president and vice-president, but as well as for 137 members of the national assembly; drawn from provinces, a national list, and six migrant spots, and for their five [[Andean Parliament]] representatives.<ref>[http://www.greenleft.org.au/node/52846 Ecuador: Correa launches re-election campaign, socialists give critical support {{!}} Green Left Weekly<!-- Bot generated title -->]</ref>

==Opinion polls==
Surveys generally attributed between 50% and 60% of the votes to President Correa. The seven other candidates lagged far behind; Correa's nearest rival, Guillermo Lasso, was credited with 10—20% of the vote.<ref>[http://www.humaniteinenglish.com/spip.php?article2238 Rafael Correa's new challenge]</ref>

